;;; A bare `_` in a rule's pattern reports a larger core than a variable used
;;; once — M1f **W29**, register row `W29/core-two-ways`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1f.5.0 (2026-11-08) from
;;; P1f.5's F3 (S1f.5.1's case, the program verbatim), filed `open` and owned
;;; by S1f.5.1, on the user's ruling of 2026-11-06 (Q-M1e2.22), which closes
;;; F3 by refusing the `_`. Its twin, `underscore-core-two-ways-twin.ein`,
;;; writes the slot `?w0`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two derivations of `holds`, `(holds b0)` by `m1` from three facts and
;;; `(holds s0)` by `s` from one; `w` reads `(holds _)` and derives `(hit w)`,
;;; and `k` concludes `(false)` from `(hit w)` and `(c x)`. Root is dead.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A bare `_` is reserved and names nothing (Q-M1e2.22), so the program is
;;; refused at load, with a line naming the place: every command that reads it
;;; exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). Written `?w0`, the twin's, root's smallest
;;; contradiction frontier is `(c x) (small s0)`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is empty, every node dead, and root's core is `(big b0) (big2 b0)
;;; (big3 b0) (c x)`, four facts where the twin's two suffice (`ein solve
;;; FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug alike).
;;; The likely cause, from these runs and not traced in the code (S1f.5.1's
;;; step 1 traces it): `_` binds nothing, so `w`'s second match looks like its
;;; first and its derivation is not recorded.

(relation holds T) (relation hit T) (relation big T) (relation big2 T) (relation big3 T) (relation small T) (relation c T)
(big b0) (big2 b0) (big3 b0)
(rule m1 () :match (and (big ?x) (big2 ?x) (big3 ?x)) :assert (holds ?x))
(rule w () :match (holds _) :assert (hit w))
(rule k () :match (and (hit w) (c x)) :assert (false))
(rule s () :match (small ?x) :assert (holds ?x))
(c x) (small s0)
